+**C++ member calls.** A member call (`e.start()`, `p->start()`, `h.engine.start()`) stores its receiver the same way, one step per `.` or `->` (`T:Holder|.engine|.start`; `T:` is any class, struct or typedef name, the namespace dropped). The base is the declared type of a local, parameter (`Pump*`, `const Pump&`) or global, what `auto` is initialized with (`Engine()`, `Engine{}`, `new Pump`, `make_unique<Pump>()`, `static_cast<T*>(...)`, a call of a function with a known return type), a cast, or for `this` and a bare name that is no local the enclosing class. A smart pointer (`unique_ptr<Pump>`, `P:Pump`) reaches its pointee through `->` and `*` only; `p.reset()` is the pointer's own and resolves to no project method. Methods are members of their class (a header declaration and an out-of-line definition count as one: the nearest to the call wins); when the receiver's class is known and has the method, the call is certain. A receiver whose type cannot be told, or a class without that method (it may be inherited), is scored by name as before and stays a guess.
